#
# The repository's Bazel entry point.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACED. A 34-byte file reading `# Scaffold file: tools/dev/bazelw`, without an
# executable bit, which ci/presubmit/pipeline.py invokes as a program:
#
#     return run([str(REPO / "tools/dev/bazelw"), "test", *targets])
#
# That call raises PermissionError. Nothing had noticed because presubmit.yml runs
# `pipeline.py --static-only`, which returns before reaching that line, and because until the
# rules_go/Go 1.26 fix no Go target could be built by Bazel at all — so the lane that would
# have hit it was not worth turning on. Both of those are now false.
#
# WHY A WRAPPER RATHER THAN CALLING bazel DIRECTLY. Two reasons, and neither is style:
#
#   1. .bazelversion pins 8.4.2 because rules_go does not load under Bazel 9. A developer with
#      Bazel 9 on PATH gets a Starlark error naming a file nobody in this repository wrote.
#      bazelisk reads the pin and fetches the right launcher; this script prefers it.
#   2. Bazel resolves the workspace from the working directory. `pipeline.py` already passes
#      cwd=REPO, but a person running this from a subdirectory should get the same build, so
#      the cd here is not redundant with that.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: inject flags. No --config=ci, no default target set. The
# caller chooses its configuration — .github/workflows/release.yml passes `--config=ci`
# explicitly, and a wrapper that silently added it would mean the command a developer copies
# out of a failing CI log behaves differently when they run it. A launcher shim that changes
# the build is not a shim.

set -euo pipefail

repo_root="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)"
cd -- "${repo_root}"

# bazelisk first: it reads .bazelversion and downloads that exact launcher.
if command -v bazelisk >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  exec bazelisk "$@"
fi

# Plain `bazel` is an acceptable fallback ONLY because the real launcher also honours
# .bazelversion — it refuses to run the wrong version rather than silently using it. What it
# cannot do is fetch the right one, so the failure is "requires Bazel 8.4.2 but it wasn't
# found", which names the problem and the fix.
if command -v bazel >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  exec bazel "$@"
fi

cat >&2 <<'ERROR'
error: neither bazelisk nor bazel is on PATH.

The development shells in flake.nix provide bazelisk:

    tools/dev/nixw develop          # or: tools/dev/nixw develop .#ci
    direnv allow         # if you would rather have it on every shell in this tree

CI installs it with bazel-contrib/setup-bazel, which reads .bazelversion the same way.
ERROR
exit 127
